      { path: "references/scripts/guard-fix-authoring.md", content: `# AUTHORING guard-fix.mjs — the per-app deterministic fix-gate

> LOAD WHEN: adding a check to the build's fix-gate (the check-ratchet fires — a defect was
> fixed, so a permanent driven check joins the gate the same round). \`guard.sh fix <wt> <url>\`
> runs the file this guide authors. One check per fixed defect; each asserts the *visible
> outcome* of the fix on the served app — never the fixer's word.

---

## File skeleton

\`\`\`js
// Deterministic FIX gate — drives the live app and mechanically asserts each CONFIRMED
// defect from the fix bundle is actually gone. Exit 0 = every check green.
import { readFileSync, readdirSync, statSync } from 'fs';
import { join } from 'path';
const [WT, URL] = process.argv.slice(2);
if (!WT || !URL) { console.error('usage: guard-fix.mjs <worktree> <url>'); process.exit(2); }
const PW = \`\${WT}/node_modules/playwright/index.js\`;
let FAIL = 0;
const red = (m, ev) => { console.log(\`RED   \${m}\${ev ? ' :: ' + ev : ''}\`); FAIL = 1; };
const grn = (m) => console.log(\`GREEN \${m}\`);
const HARD = setTimeout(() => { console.log('RED   guard-fix self-timeout'); process.exit(1); }, 900000);
let chromium;
try { const mod = await import(PW); chromium = (mod.default || mod).chromium; }
catch (e) { red('playwright import', String(e).slice(0,120)); process.exit(1); }

async function run() {
  // static checks first (fast, no browser) …
  const b = await chromium.launch();
  // … driven checks, one isolated block each …
  await b.close();
}
try { await run(); } catch (e) { red('guard-fix fatal', String(e).slice(0,150)); }
clearTimeout(HARD);
console.log(FAIL ? '\\nFIX-GATE FAIL — not done' : '\\nFIX-GATE PASS');
process.exit(FAIL ? 1 : 0);
\`\`\`

The final line prints \`FIX-GATE PASS\`/\`FIX-GATE FAIL\` — \`guard.sh fix\` greps for it; keep
the strings exact.

---

## Check anatomy (driven checks)

Every driven check is one self-contained block with this shape:

\`\`\`js
// --- <gateId> (<ledger id / defect>): <one-line claim the check falsifies> ---
{ const pg = await (await b.newContext({ viewport:{width:1512,height:900}, deviceScaleFactor:2 })).newPage();
  await pg.goto(URL, { waitUntil:'domcontentloaded' });
  await pg.waitForSelector('[data-mustsee]', { timeout: 9000 }).catch(()=>{});
  await pg.waitForTimeout(2500);                       // settle for canvas/animation
  try {
    /* drive → observe → assert IDENTITY with evidence */
  } catch (e) { red('<gateId>: drive error', String(e).slice(0,100)); }
  await pg.close(); }
\`\`\`

The rules, each one a paid-for failure mode:

1. **Isolated context per check.** A fresh \`newContext\`/\`newPage\` per block — shared pages
   leak state between checks (a prior check's mutation poisons the next one's baseline).
2. **\`domcontentloaded\`, never \`networkidle\`.** A WebGL canvas or long-poll never goes
   network-idle; the wait hangs to the harness watchdog. Readiness = \`waitForSelector\` on a
   real element (\`[data-mustsee]\`), then a short fixed settle.
3. **Assert with evidence.** Every \`red()\` carries the observed value (\`ev\`), not just the
   verdict — the executor fixes from the evidence string. Assert identity, not shape (L02):
   the value equals the expectation / the state changed to X, never "an element exists".
4. **Both poles.** A check asserting a transition captures BEFORE and AFTER the action and
   asserts the delta — so a shared/seeded store that is already in the end state cannot
   false-fail it, and an initial state that already satisfies the assertion cannot
   false-pass it. (Example class: "one click must NOT commit" — read the committed flag
   before the click; only a before=false → after=true transition is the defect.)
5. **State-aware reasoned GREEN.** When a check's precondition is absent by design (the
   record is sealed so the control is *correctly* disabled; the optional surface is not in
   this dataset), print a GREEN that says *why it did not run* — never a silent pass, never
   a RED for a legitimate state. A skipped check must be distinguishable from a passed one
   in the output.
6. **Bounded retries on flaky physical gestures.** Drags/pointer sequences get a small
   retry loop (≤3) with a settle between attempts — then a hard verdict. Never unbounded.
7. **Adversarial variants are separate checks.** The paced drag AND the fast drag; portrait
   AND landscape; light AND dark when the defect was theme-scoped. A fix that holds only on
   the paced/portrait/light variant is not fixed.
8. **Self-cleanup + hard exit.** \`b.close()\` in the run tail, the global \`HARD\` timeout so
   the script ALWAYS returns, \`process.exit\` with the real status. \`guard.sh\` must never
   hang on this file.

## Static checks

Defects provable from the source/CSS (a banned class still present, a bare hex where a
token belongs, a font-size below the legibility floor, a file over the line-limit) get a
file-scan check instead of a browser drive — same \`red/grn\` contract, runs first, costs
milliseconds. Walk the target directory excluding \`node_modules\`/\`.git\`/\`dist\`; report
\`file:line\` in the evidence.

## Measurement math

- **Contrast:** compute real WCAG ratios (relative luminance, the 4.5:1 / 3:1 thresholds by
  size+weight) — never eyeball, never trust a library's "accessible" boolean. \`capture.mjs\`
  carries the reference implementation, including oklab()/oklch() resolution in-page (canvas alone
  mis-resolves those tokens and yields a false ~1:1; the script does not use canvas alone). *Superseded
  2026-07: accent-element contrast fails no longer need a shot-confirm — they are real findings.*
  \`color-mix()\` with unresolved custom properties is skipped rather than guessed.
- **Geometry:** \`getBoundingClientRect\` deltas with an explicit tolerance (±1px for
  layout, a stated px budget for landing accuracy) — the tolerance is part of the check's
  contract, printed in the GREEN line.
- **Money/derived values:** re-derive the expectation in the check (\`net = adjusted −
  deductible\`) and compare — never assert the on-screen number against itself.

## data-testid discipline — a BUILD deliverable

Checks address elements by \`data-testid\` (and act-critical elements by \`[data-mustsee]\`).
The executor adds these hooks AS PART OF the feature, not as test scaffolding bolted on
later: stable ids on every interactive control and every asserted value. A check that has
to address \`.class > div:nth-child(3)\` breaks on the next refactor and the gate goes silent
— brittle selectors are a gate defect (fix the build to expose the id, then the check).

## Meta-gate obligation

Every new check is proven against its known-bad state once before it counts: re-introduce
the defect (or check out the pre-fix commit / temporarily invert the CSS), run the gate,
watch THIS check go RED on that exact case, restore. A fix-gate check that has never been
red is decoration (L01). Record the observed red in the ledger row's evidence cell.` },
      { path: "references/scripts/guard.sh", content: `#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Deterministic guard gate for an app build (generic — pair with a per-app guard-fix.mjs).
# HARD-BLOCKS a build on structural violations no human should have to catch by eye:
#   tsc · no-any · file line-limits · no word-gates · build · no-scroll@1512x900.
# Usage:
#   guard.sh structural <worktree> <screen-glob>     # tsc + no-any + line-limits + no word-gates (fast)
#   guard.sh build      <worktree>                   # vite build
#   guard.sh scroll     <url> [w] [h] [worktree]     # headless no-scroll assertion
#   guard.sh fix        <worktree> <url>             # the PER-APP fix-gate (guard-fix.mjs, BESIDE this file)
#   guard.sh perf       <worktree> [url]             # measured-speed gate vs the PRD \`Perf-budget:\` line; writes docs/PERF.md
#   guard.sh reactivity <worktree> <url>             # driven R1–R6 gate vs the PRD \`Reactivity:\` row; writes docs/REACTIVITY.md
#   guard.sh divergence <worktree>                   # serving-copy gate vs the PRD \`Read-path:\` row; writes docs/DIVERGENCE.md
#   guard.sh all        <worktree> <screen-glob> <url>
# \`all\` runs ALL SEVEN layers: structural, build, scroll, fix, perf, reactivity, divergence. A per-app
# guard-fix.mjs that does not exist yet is reported as a SKIPPED layer, never silently passed and never
# counted as a failure.
# Exit 0 = all green. Non-zero = a gate is RED (prints which + why). No masking of stderr.
set -uo pipefail
FAIL=0
red(){ printf '\\033[31mRED  \\033[0m %s\\n' "$1"; FAIL=1; }
grn(){ printf '\\033[32mGREEN\\033[0m %s\\n' "$1"; }

COMP_LIMIT=200   # .tsx component hard limit
MOD_LIMIT=120    # any other .ts module hard limit

structural(){
  local wt="$1" glob="$2"
  cd "$wt" || { red "worktree not found: $wt"; return; }
  # DEFAULT GLOB: the lane's WHOLE assigned file set, not a hand-typed screen list. A
  # caller-supplied non-recursive \`ls\` pattern silently excluded \`server/**\`, so the gate reported
  # clean over files it had never opened — the check everyone assumed was running could not run.
  if [ -z "$glob" ]; then
    glob="$(find src server app lib -type f \\
              \\( -name '*.ts' -o -name '*.tsx' -o -name '*.mjs' -o -name '*.js' -o -name '*.css' \\) \\
              2>/dev/null | tr '\\n' ' ')"
    [ -n "$glob" ] && printf '\\033[36m›\\033[0m no glob passed — defaulting to the whole source set (%s files)\\n' \\
      "$(printf '%s' "$glob" | wc -w | tr -d ' ')"
  fi

  # CHECK 1 — typecheck
  if npx tsc -b >/tmp/guard-tsc.log 2>&1; then grn "tsc -b clean"; else red "tsc -b failed (see /tmp/guard-tsc.log)"; tail -5 /tmp/guard-tsc.log; fi

  # CHECK 2 — no \`any\` / no casts in the screen files
  local anyhits
  anyhits=$(grep -rnE ':\\s*any\\b|as any\\b|as unknown\\b|@ts-(ignore|expect-error)|eslint-disable' $glob 2>&1 | grep -v ':0:' || true)
  if [ -z "$anyhits" ]; then grn "no any/casts/suppressions in screen files"; else red "any/cast/suppression found:"; echo "$anyhits" | head -20; fi

  # CHECK 3 — file line-limits (the monolith gate)
  local over=""
  while IFS= read -r f; do
    [ -f "$f" ] || continue
    local n lim; n=$(wc -l < "$f" | tr -d ' ')
    # The monolith gate targets LOGIC modules (.tsx components ≤200, .ts modules ≤120).
    # Stylesheets (.css) are not logic modules — a long screen stylesheet is normal; skip them.
    # \`.mjs\` is a logic module and takes the module limit EXPLICITLY — it used to reach the same
    # limit only by falling through the default arm, which is a limit nobody could read off the code.
    case "$f" in *.css) continue;; *.tsx) lim=$COMP_LIMIT;; *.mjs|*.js|*.ts) lim=$MOD_LIMIT;; *) lim=$MOD_LIMIT;; esac
    if [ "$n" -gt "$lim" ]; then over="$over  $f: $n lines (limit $lim)\\n"; fi
  done < <(printf '%s\\n' $glob)
  if [ -z "$over" ]; then grn "all files within line-limits (.tsx ≤$COMP_LIMIT · .ts/.mjs/.js ≤$MOD_LIMIT · .css exempt)"; else red "line-limit violated (.tsx ≤$COMP_LIMIT · .ts/.mjs/.js ≤$MOD_LIMIT):"; printf "$over"; fi

  # CHECK 4 — the WORD-GATE scan: the static half of R2 (frontend-architecture.md §6,
  # "first paint is content-shaped, not a word"). A \`return <tag>Loading…</tag>\` early
  # exit IS the blank region §6 was written to stop, and it is the cheapest thing in the
  # whole rubric to catch — it is a text node in the source, visible without a browser.
  # The driven half (\`guard.sh reactivity\`) catches the same defect however it is
  # authored; this arm catches it at authoring time, before anything is served.
  # A skeleton COMPONENT (\`return <Skeleton rows={8}/>\`) has no text child and never matches,
  # and neither does a QUOTED string — \`t("loading.label")\` and \`aria-label="Loading"\` are not
  # word-gates, they are copy. Only a JSX TEXT CHILD counts, in its three authored shapes:
  #   A  return <div …>Loading…</div>            single-line early return
  #   B  …>Loading {x}…</div>                    a complete element with a loading text child
  #   C  a line that IS the text child           the multi-line form, unquoted, not a comment
  local WG='Loading|Fetching|Please wait|Chargement|Cargando|Caricamento|Carregando'
  local words
  words=$( { grep -rnE "return[[:space:]]*\\(?[[:space:]]*<[^>]*>[[:space:]]*\\{?[[:space:]]*($WG)" $glob 2>&1;
             grep -rnE ">[[:space:]]*($WG)[^<\\"']*<" $glob 2>&1;
             grep -rnE "^[[:space:]]*($WG)([[:space:]]*\\{[^\\"']*\\})?[[:space:]]*(\\.\\.\\.|…)?[[:space:]]*$" $glob 2>&1; } \\
           | grep -vE ':[0-9]+:[[:space:]]*(//|\\*|#)' | grep -v ':0:' | sort -u -t: -k1,2 || true)
  if [ -z "$words" ]; then grn "no word-gate early returns (R2 static arm)"; else red "word-gate early return — first paint is a WORD, not content (frontend-architecture.md §6 R2):"; echo "$words" | head -20; fi
}

build(){
  cd "$1" || { red "worktree not found: $1"; return; }
  if npx vite build >/tmp/guard-build.log 2>&1; then grn "vite build clean"; else red "vite build failed (see /tmp/guard-build.log)"; tail -8 /tmp/guard-build.log; fi
}

scroll(){
  local url="$1" w="\${2:-1512}" h="\${3:-900}"
  # Resolve Playwright the way capture.mjs does — a caller outside the worktree must still work.
  local wt="\${4:-}"
  if [ -z "$wt" ]; then
    for c in "$PWD" "$HOME/.nexus-tools" "$(cd "$(dirname "\${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)"; do
      [ -f "$c/node_modules/playwright/index.js" ] && { wt="$c"; break; }
    done
  fi
  [ -z "$wt" ] && wt="$(pwd)"
  cat > /tmp/guard-scroll.mjs <<JS
import pkg from '\${wt}/node_modules/playwright/index.js';
const { chromium } = pkg;
const b = await chromium.launch();
const p = await b.newPage({ viewport: { width: \${w}, height: \${h} }, deviceScaleFactor: 2 });
await p.goto('\${url}'); await p.waitForTimeout(1800);
const r = await p.evaluate(() => {
  const sh = document.scrollingElement.scrollHeight, ih = innerHeight, iw = innerWidth;
  const nodes = [...document.querySelectorAll('[data-mustsee]')];
  const bad = [];
  for (const el of nodes) {
    const b = el.getBoundingClientRect();
    const inView = b.top >= -1 && b.left >= -1 && b.bottom <= ih + 1 && b.right <= iw + 1 && b.width > 0 && b.height > 0;
    const cx = b.left + b.width / 2, cy = b.top + b.height / 2;
    const hit = document.elementFromPoint(cx, cy);
    const hittable = hit && (el.contains(hit) || hit.contains(el));
    if (!inView || !hittable) bad.push({ sel: el.getAttribute('data-mustsee'), inView, hittable, bottom: Math.round(b.bottom) });
  }
  return { scrollHeight: sh, innerHeight: ih, fits: sh <= ih + 1, mustsee_count: nodes.length, mustsee_ok: nodes.length > 0 && bad.length === 0, mustsee_bad: bad };
});
console.log(JSON.stringify(r));
await b.close();
JS
  local out; out=$(node /tmp/guard-scroll.mjs 2>&1)
  echo "$out"
  if echo "$out" | grep -q '"fits":true'; then grn "no page-scroll @ \${w}x\${h}"; else red "PAGE SCROLLS @ \${w}x\${h} — content exceeds viewport"; fi
  # Visibility gate: every [data-mustsee] element must be fully in-viewport AND hit-testable
  # (catches the clipped-inside-a-fixed-column false-green: page doesn't scroll but content is hidden).
  if echo "$out" | grep -q '"mustsee_ok":true'; then grn "all [data-mustsee] elements visible (not clipped/covered)"; \\
  elif echo "$out" | grep -q '"mustsee_count":0'; then red "no [data-mustsee] elements marked — the build must tag the act-critical elements (Submit, rollup total, coverage) so visibility is enforceable"; \\
  else red "a [data-mustsee] element is CLIPPED or off-viewport @ \${w}x\${h}:"; echo "$out" | grep -o '"mustsee_bad":\\[[^]]*\\]'; fi
}

fix(){
  # Deterministic per-defect FIX gate — drives the served app and asserts each confirmed
  # bundle defect is actually gone (seal · density · scroll768 · noClSeg · sheetMobile).
  local wt="$1" url="$2"
  local here; here="$(cd "$(dirname "\${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)"
  if [ ! -f "$here/guard-fix.mjs" ]; then
    printf '\\033[33m○\\033[0m fix-gate SKIPPED — no guard-fix.mjs beside guard.sh (%s).\\n' "$here"
    printf '  It is authored PER APP (see guard-fix-authoring.md) and MUST be written to that exact path;\\n'
    printf '  guard.sh resolves it from its own directory, so a fix-gate anywhere else is never run.\\n'
    return
  fi
  local out; out=$(node "$here/guard-fix.mjs" "$wt" "$url" 2>&1)
  echo "$out"
  echo "$out" | grep -q 'FIX-GATE PASS' && grn "deterministic fix-gate PASS" || red "deterministic fix-gate FAIL"
}

perf(){
  # Layer 5 — the measured-speed gate (W42). Reads the PRD's machine row
  #   \`Perf-budget: … [· bundle <N>kb] [· route-read <N>ms cold / <N>ms warm]\`
  # measures the SERVED artifacts against it, and writes docs/PERF.md — the evidence
  # artifact the PERFEV lifecycle check reads (non-empty, digit-bearing). Deterministic
  # verdicts only where a budget component is parseable + measurable here:
  #   bundle    — dist/ total KB vs \`bundle <N>kb\`
  #   route-read WARM — median of repeat requests vs \`… <N>ms warm\` (needs [url])
  # A true COLD read cannot be forced generically — the first-request time is recorded,
  # labeled, and NOT gated (the perf council seat + R7 journeys own cold). No budget
  # line in docs/PRD.md = reasoned GREEN (prints why, writes nothing): a skipped check
  # must never look like a passed one.
  local wt="$1" url="\${2:-}"
  cd "$wt" || { red "worktree not found: $wt"; return; }
  local bl
  bl=$(grep -m1 "Perf-budget:" docs/PRD.md 2>&1 | grep -E '[0-9]' || true)
  if [ -z "$bl" ]; then
    grn "perf gate: reasoned GREEN — docs/PRD.md carries no digit-bearing Perf-budget: line (nothing to gate against; declare one to arm this layer)"
    return
  fi
  local out="# PERF — measured-speed evidence ($(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M'))\\n\\nBudget: \${bl}\\n"
  # bundle component
  local bkb mkb
  bkb=$(printf '%s' "$bl" | grep -oiE 'bundle[^0-9]*[0-9]+[[:space:]]*kb' | grep -oE '[0-9]+' | head -1 || true)
  if [ -d dist ]; then
    mkb=$(du -sk dist 2>&1 | cut -f1)
    out="\${out}\\nBundle (dist/): \${mkb} KB"
    if [ -n "$bkb" ]; then
      if [ "$mkb" -le "$bkb" ]; then grn "bundle \${mkb}KB within budget \${bkb}KB"; out="\${out} vs budget \${bkb} KB — PASS"; \\
      else red "bundle \${mkb}KB EXCEEDS budget \${bkb}KB"; out="\${out} vs budget \${bkb} KB — FAIL"; fi
    else out="\${out} (no bundle component in the budget line — measured, not gated)"; fi
  else
    out="\${out}\\nBundle: dist/ absent (run \\\`guard.sh build\\\` first) — SKIPPED"
    printf '\\033[33m○\\033[0m perf bundle SKIPPED — no dist/ (build first)\\n'
  fi
  # route-read warm component
  local warmms t1 t2 t3 t4 med
  warmms=$(printf '%s' "$bl" | grep -oiE '[0-9]+[[:space:]]*ms[[:space:]]*warm' | grep -oE '[0-9]+' | head -1 || true)
  if [ -n "$url" ]; then
    t1=$(curl -so /dev/null -w '%{time_total}' "$url" 2>&1 || echo "")
    t2=$(curl -so /dev/null -w '%{time_total}' "$url" 2>&1 || echo "")
    t3=$(curl -so /dev/null -w '%{time_total}' "$url" 2>&1 || echo "")
    t4=$(curl -so /dev/null -w '%{time_total}' "$url" 2>&1 || echo "")
    if [ -n "$t2" ] && [ -n "$t3" ] && [ -n "$t4" ]; then
      med=$(printf '%s\\n%s\\n%s\\n' "$t2" "$t3" "$t4" | sort -n | sed -n 2p | awk '{printf "%d", $1*1000}')
      out="\${out}\\nRoute read \${url}: first-request $(printf '%s' "$t1" | awk '{printf "%d", $1*1000}')ms (recorded, NOT a cold measurement — the seat/journeys own cold) · warm median \${med}ms"
      if [ -n "$warmms" ]; then
        if [ "$med" -le "$warmms" ]; then grn "warm route-read \${med}ms within budget \${warmms}ms"; out="\${out} vs budget \${warmms}ms warm — PASS"; \\
        else red "warm route-read \${med}ms EXCEEDS budget \${warmms}ms"; out="\${out} vs budget \${warmms}ms warm — FAIL"; fi
      else out="\${out} (no warm component parseable — measured, not gated)"; fi
    else
      red "perf route-read: url unreachable (\${url})"; out="\${out}\\nRoute read: URL unreachable — FAIL"
    fi
  else
    out="\${out}\\nRoute read: no url passed — SKIPPED (pass the served URL to gate warm latency)"
    printf '\\033[33m○\\033[0m perf route-read SKIPPED — no url passed\\n'
  fi
  mkdir -p docs && printf "\${out}\\n" > docs/PERF.md
  grn "evidence written: docs/PERF.md"
}

reactivity(){
  # Layer 6 — the driven R1–R6 gate. Reads the PRD's machine row
  #   \`Reactivity: <route> R1,R2,… · <route> R…\`
  # drives each declared surface in a real browser, and writes docs/REACTIVITY.md —
  # the evidence artifact the REACTEV lifecycle check reads. Same shape as layer 5:
  # a declared budget/row is what ARMS the layer, and no row = reasoned GREEN.
  #
  # The driver is reactivity.mjs BESIDE this file (resolved from $here, the \`fix\`
  # precedent) — unlike guard-fix.mjs it is generic and always ships, so its absence
  # is a broken install, not an un-authored gate.
  local wt="$1" url="\${2:-}"
  local here; here="$(cd "$(dirname "\${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)"
  if [ ! -f "$here/reactivity.mjs" ]; then
    red "reactivity gate: reactivity.mjs is missing beside guard.sh ($here) — broken install, not a pass"
    return
  fi
  if [ -z "$url" ]; then
    printf '\\033[33m○\\033[0m reactivity SKIPPED — no url passed (the predicates are DRIVEN; pass the served URL)\\n'
    return
  fi
  local out; out=$(node "$here/reactivity.mjs" "$wt" "$url" 2>&1); local rc=$?
  echo "$out"
  if [ "$rc" -eq 0 ]; then grn "reactivity gate green (see docs/REACTIVITY.md)"; \\
  elif [ "$rc" -eq 2 ]; then red "reactivity gate could not RUN (rc=2) — an unrunnable gate is not a green"; \\
  else red "reactivity gate FAIL — a declared R1–R6 predicate is violated on a served surface"; fi
}

divergence(){
  # Layer 7 — the serving-copy correctness gate. Reads the PRD's machine row
  #   \`Read-path: <surface> projection|direct|connector · host <where> · divergence <check or none>\`
  # and enforces that a PROJECTION — a second copy of the canonical store — carries a
  # check that exists, runs, and passes. It writes docs/DIVERGENCE.md.
  #
  # performance-playbook.md D1 required this journey and then recorded that no guard.sh
  # layer read it, which made "divergence <check>" a field nobody could fail. This layer
  # is the wall that line described as unbuilt.
  #
  # It does NOT compare the copies itself — only the app knows how. It enforces that the
  # comparison exists. That is the same division of labour as \`fix\`: generic gate,
  # per-app check.
  #
  # No url argument: divergence is decided against the stores, not a browser. That also
  # makes this the one driven layer with no Playwright dependency, so it runs anywhere.
  local wt="$1"
  local here; here="$(cd "$(dirname "\${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)"
  if [ ! -f "$here/divergence.mjs" ]; then
    red "divergence gate: divergence.mjs is missing beside guard.sh ($here) — broken install, not a pass"
    return
  fi
  local out; out=$(node "$here/divergence.mjs" "$wt" 2>&1); local rc=$?
  echo "$out"
  if [ "$rc" -eq 0 ]; then grn "divergence gate green (see docs/DIVERGENCE.md)"; \\
  elif [ "$rc" -eq 2 ]; then red "divergence gate could not RUN (rc=2) — an unrunnable gate is not a green"; \\
  else red "divergence gate FAIL — a declared serving copy is unchecked, or its check says the copy lies"; fi
}

case "\${1:-}" in
  structural) structural "$2" "\${3:-}";;
  build)      build "$2";;
  scroll)     scroll "$2" "\${3:-1512}" "\${4:-900}";;
  fix)        fix "$2" "$3";;
  perf)       perf "$2" "\${3:-}";;
  reactivity) reactivity "$2" "\${3:-}";;
  divergence) divergence "$2";;
  all)        structural "$2" "\${3:-}"; build "$2"; scroll "$4" 1512 900 "$2"; fix "$2" "$4"; perf "$2" "$4"; reactivity "$2" "$4"; divergence "$2";;
  *) echo "usage: guard.sh {structural <wt> [glob: defaults to the whole source set]|build <wt>|scroll <url> [w h] [wt]|fix <wt> <url>|perf <wt> [url]|reactivity <wt> <url>|divergence <wt>|all <wt> <glob> <url>}"; exit 2;;
esac

[ "$FAIL" -eq 0 ] && { echo; grn "GUARD PASS"; exit 0; } || { echo; red "GUARD FAIL — build is NOT done"; exit 1; }` },
      { path: "references/scripts/reactivity.mjs", content: `#!/usr/bin/env node
/**
 * reactivity.mjs — the driven gate for the R1–R6 acceptance predicates
 * (\`frontend-architecture.md\` §6). Called by \`guard.sh reactivity\`, never by hand.
 *
 *   node reactivity.mjs <worktree> <urlbase> [--json]
 *   node reactivity.mjs --self-test
 *
 * Why this exists: §6 says a data surface with NO asserted predicate is \`❌ untested\`,
 * "the same class as a UI feature with no primitive" — and then delegated R1–R6 to a
 * prose instruction in an agent file. R7 got a gate in W42 (a machine row, a guard
 * layer, a Stop check, a registry row); R1–R6 stayed on rung 3, which
 * \`gates-and-ledger.md\` says fails silently and unevenly. This is that same gate,
 * ported, for the predicates a browser can actually decide.
 *
 * WHAT IT READS. The PRD's machine row, the \`Perf-budget:\` precedent:
 *
 *     Reactivity: /#/o/companies R1,R2,R4,R5,R6 · /#/o/deals R1,R2
 *
 * A route, then the predicates that surface carries. No row = reasoned GREEN (prints
 * why, writes nothing): a skipped check must never look like a passed one.
 *
 * WHAT THE APP DECLARES. Two attributes, the \`[data-mustsee]\` precedent — the build
 * tags, the gate drives what is tagged, and ZERO tagged is a RED rather than a pass.
 *   [data-surface]         the content root of a data surface (R1, R2)
 *   [data-surface-filter]  that surface's filter/search input   (R1-filter, R4, R5, R6)
 *
 * WHAT IT DECIDES, and what it deliberately does not — read \`frontend-architecture.md\`
 * §6a before trusting a green. R3 is RECORDED, never gated. R2 is gated against the
 * word-gate half of its predicate only. Every partial is printed as its own row.
 *
 * Exit 0 = no FAIL row. Exit 1 = at least one FAIL. Exit 2 = the checker itself is broken.
 */
import fs from 'node:fs';
import path from 'node:path';
import http from 'node:http';
import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';

const HERE = path.dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url));
const argv = process.argv.slice(2);
const has = (n) => argv.includes(n);

// ── dependency resolution: find Playwright without being TOLD where it is (capture.mjs) ──
const NEXUS_TOOLS = path.join(process.env.HOME || process.env.USERPROFILE || '', '.nexus-tools');
const PW_CANDIDATES = () => [
  process.env.PW,
  path.join(NEXUS_TOOLS, 'node_modules/playwright/index.js'),
  path.join(HERE, 'node_modules/playwright/index.js'),
  path.join(process.cwd(), 'node_modules/playwright/index.js'),
  'playwright',
].filter(Boolean);
const PW_INSTALL_HINT = \`mkdir -p \${NEXUS_TOOLS} && cd \${NEXUS_TOOLS} && npm init -y && npm i playwright && npx playwright install chromium\`;
async function resolveChromium() {
  const tried = [];
  for (const c of PW_CANDIDATES()) {
    try {
      const m = await import(c);
      const chromium = (m.default || m).chromium;
      if (chromium) return { chromium, from: c };
      tried.push(\`\${c} (no chromium export)\`);
    } catch { tried.push(c); }
  }
  return { chromium: null, tried };
}

// ── verdict rows ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// PASS / FAIL are the gated verdicts. RECORDED is measured-and-printed with no
// verdict (the \`guard.sh perf\` cold-read precedent). NOT-DRIVEN is a reasoned
// GREEN: the check states why it could not run, and never passes silently.
const rows = [];
const R = (route, pred, verdict, detail) => rows.push({ route, pred, verdict, detail });

/** The declared row → [{ route, preds:Set }]. */
export function parseReactivityRow(text) {
  const ln = (text || '').split('\\n').find((l) => l.includes('Reactivity:') && /\\bR[1-6]\\b/.test(l));
  if (!ln) return null;
  const body = ln.slice(ln.indexOf('Reactivity:') + 'Reactivity:'.length);
  const out = [];
  for (const chunk of body.split('·')) {
    const preds = [...chunk.matchAll(/\\bR([1-6])\\b/g)].map((m) => \`R\${m[1]}\`);
    if (!preds.length) continue;
    const route = chunk.trim().split(/\\s+/)[0];
    if (!route || /^R[1-6]/.test(route)) continue;
    out.push({ route, preds: [...new Set(preds)] });
  }
  return out.length ? out : null;
}

// ── the in-page sampler ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Installed at document_start so the FIRST paint is sampled, not just the settled
// frame. Per animation frame it records, for every [data-surface], the descendant
// count and a text head; plus the body text head, which is how a word-gate that
// replaces the WHOLE app (rather than one surface) stays visible to R2.
const SAMPLER = \`
window.__nxR = { log: [], marks: [] };
window.__nxR.mark = (p) => window.__nxR.marks.push({ p, t: performance.now(), i: window.__nxR.log.length });
(function tick() {
  try {
    const els = document.querySelectorAll('[data-surface]');
    const marks = {};
    els.forEach((el, i) => {
      const key = el.getAttribute('data-surface') || ('#' + i);
      marks[key] = { n: el.getElementsByTagName('*').length,
                     txt: (el.textContent || '').replace(/\\\\s+/g, ' ').trim().slice(0, 120) };
    });
    // RENDERED body text only. A bare document.body.textContent also returns every
    // inline <script> source, which reads as 120 chars of JS and hides the word-gate
    // frame this sampler exists to catch.
    let body = '';
    if (document.body) for (const c of document.body.children) {
      if (!/^(SCRIPT|STYLE|TEMPLATE|NOSCRIPT|LINK|META)$/.test(c.tagName)) body += (c.textContent || '') + ' ';
    }
    window.__nxR.log.push({ t: performance.now(), marks,
      body: body.replace(/\\\\s+/g, ' ').trim().slice(0, 120),
      surfaces: els.length });
  } catch (e) { /* a sampler that throws must never take the page with it */ }
  requestAnimationFrame(tick);
})();
\`;

/** The word-gate vocabulary. A loading STATE whose entire rendered text is one of these
 *  is the "first paint is a word" defect verbatim. WORD_MAX bounds "a word, not content":
 *  a content-shaped skeleton renders either no text or far more than this. */
const WORD_RE = /^(loading|fetching|please wait|chargement|cargando|caricamento|laden|carregando)\\b/i;
const WORD_MAX = 60;
const isWordGate = (t) => !!t && t.length <= WORD_MAX && WORD_RE.test(t);

const nav = (page, route) => page.evaluate((r) => {
  window.__nxR.mark('nav:' + r);
  const before = window.__nxNavStamp;
  window.__nxNavStamp = before === undefined ? 1 : before;
  if (r.includes('#')) location.hash = r.slice(r.indexOf('#') + 1);
  else { history.pushState({}, '', r); dispatchEvent(new PopStateEvent('popstate')); }
}, route);

const readLog = (page) => page.evaluate(() => ({ log: window.__nxR.log, marks: window.__nxR.marks }));
const clearLog = (page) => page.evaluate(() => { window.__nxR.log.length = 0; window.__nxR.marks.length = 0; });

async function settle(page, ms = 1500) {
  await page.waitForFunction(
    () => document.querySelectorAll('[data-surface]').length > 0,
    null, { timeout: 15000 },
  ).catch(() => {});
  await page.waitForTimeout(ms);
}

/** min / final descendant count per surface key over a log slice. */
function counts(log) {
  const keys = new Set();
  for (const s of log) for (const k of Object.keys(s.marks)) keys.add(k);
  const out = {};
  for (const k of keys) {
    const seen = log.map((s) => s.marks[k]).filter(Boolean).map((m) => m.n);
    if (!seen.length) continue;
    out[k] = { min: Math.min(...seen), final: seen[seen.length - 1], frames: seen.length };
  }
  return out;
}

// ── the probe token: drawn from the surface's OWN visible data ─────────────────
// A fixture token would only prove the fixture matches itself. The first word of
// length >= 4 inside a tagged surface is a string the app is currently displaying,
// so a search for it must find at least the row it came from.
async function probeToken(page) {
  return page.evaluate(() => {
    const el = document.querySelector('[data-surface]');
    if (!el) return null;
    const words = (el.textContent || '').split(/[^\\p{L}\\p{N}]+/u).filter((w) => w.length >= 4);
    return words[0] || null;
  });
}
const ACCENTED = { a: 'á', e: 'é', i: 'í', o: 'ó', u: 'ú', c: 'ç', n: 'ñ' };
/** Put ONE accent on the token. Tests accented-query-finds-unaccented-data, so the
 *  arm runs on any token — it never depends on the app's data carrying diacritics. */
function accentise(tok) {
  for (let i = 0; i < tok.length; i++) {
    const a = ACCENTED[tok[i].toLowerCase()];
    if (a) return tok.slice(0, i) + a + tok.slice(i + 1);
  }
  return null;
}

async function typeFilter(page, value) {
  const ok = await page.evaluate((v) => {
    const el = document.querySelector('[data-surface-filter]');
    if (!el) return false;
    const setter = Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor(el.constructor.prototype, 'value')?.set;
    if (setter) setter.call(el, v); else el.value = v;
    el.dispatchEvent(new Event('input', { bubbles: true }));
    el.dispatchEvent(new Event('change', { bubbles: true }));
    return true;
  }, value);
  if (ok) await page.waitForTimeout(900);
  return ok;
}
const surfaceState = (page) => page.evaluate(() => {
  const el = document.querySelector('[data-surface]');
  return el ? { n: el.getElementsByTagName('*').length,
                txt: (el.textContent || '').replace(/\\s+/g, ' ').trim() } : null;
});

// ── the drive ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
async function driveRoute(page, urlbase, entry, all) {
  const { route, preds } = entry;
  const want = (p) => preds.includes(p);
  const reqs = [];
  const onReq = (r) => reqs.push({ url: r.url(), start: Date.now() });
  const onRes = (r) => { const e = reqs.find((x) => x.url === r.url() && !x.end); if (e) e.end = Date.now(); };
  page.on('request', onReq);
  page.on('response', onRes);

  await page.goto(urlbase + route, { waitUntil: 'commit' }).catch(() => {});
  await settle(page);
  const first = await readLog(page);
  page.off('request', onReq);
  page.off('response', onRes);

  if (!first.log.length || !first.log[first.log.length - 1].surfaces) {
    R(route, 'ALL', 'FAIL', 'no [data-surface] element on this route — the build must tag each '
      + 'data surface\\'s content root; zero tagged is a RED, not a pass (the [data-mustsee] rule)');
    return;
  }

  // ── R2 — the word-gate half of "first paint is content-shaped, not a word" ──
  if (want('R2')) {
    const hits = [];
    for (const s of first.log) {
      for (const [k, m] of Object.entries(s.marks)) if (isWordGate(m.txt)) hits.push(\`\${k}: "\${m.txt}"\`);
      if (!s.surfaces && isWordGate(s.body)) hits.push(\`<body>: "\${s.body}"\`);
    }
    if (hits.length) {
      R(route, 'R2', 'FAIL', \`first paint is a WORD, not content — \${[...new Set(hits)][0]} \`
        + \`(seen in \${hits.length} sampled frame(s))\`);
    } else {
      R(route, 'R2', 'PASS', 'no loading-word frame during the cold load');
    }
    R(route, 'R2-shape', 'NOT-DRIVEN', 'the "content-shaped" half is not decided here — no '
      + 'app-independent definition of a content-shaped skeleton exists that does not either '
      + 'invent a threshold or contradict the layer-3 no-scroll assertion. Rung 3, by design');
  }

  // ── R3 — RECORDED, never gated ──
  if (want('R3')) {
    const app = reqs.filter((r) => r.end && !/\\.(js|css|png|svg|woff2?|ico|map)(\\?|$)/.test(r.url));
    let serial = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < app.length; i++) {
      for (let j = 0; j < app.length; j++) if (i !== j && app[j].end && app[i].start >= app[j].end) { serial++; break; }
    }
    R(route, 'R3', 'RECORDED', \`\${app.length} non-asset request(s) on route load, \${serial} of them \`
      + 'started after another had already finished. NOT gated: this gate cannot tell a legitimately '
      + 'DEPENDENT chain from a serial waterfall, and a version driven by an author declaration '
      + 'passes by under-declaring. Read the chain, do not read a verdict');
  }

  const tok = await probeToken(page);
  const hasFilter = await page.evaluate(() => !!document.querySelector('[data-surface-filter]'));
  const needsFilter = ['R4', 'R5', 'R6'].filter(want).concat(want('R1') ? ['R1-filter'] : []);
  if (needsFilter.length && !hasFilter) {
    for (const p of needsFilter) {
      R(route, p, 'FAIL', 'no [data-surface-filter] on a route that declares this predicate — tag the '
        + 'surface\\'s filter control, or drop the predicate from the PRD row');
    }
  }

  // ── R1 — never-blank-what-was-shown ──
  if (want('R1')) {
    // axis A: client-side nav away and back. Threshold-free: the surface may never dip
    // below BOTH what it had and what it ends with — that dip IS the blank frame.
    const other = all.find((e) => e.route !== route);
    if (!other) {
      R(route, 'R1-nav', 'NOT-DRIVEN', 'the nav axis needs a second declared route to leave for — '
        + 'declare one in the PRD Reactivity row');
    } else {
      const before = counts(first.log.slice(-8));
      await clearLog(page);
      await nav(page, other.route);
      await page.waitForTimeout(1200);
      const stamp = await page.evaluate(() => window.__nxR ? window.__nxR.log.length : -1);
      if (stamp <= 0) {
        R(route, 'R1-nav', 'NOT-DRIVEN', \`leaving for \${other.route} reloaded the document, so there \`
          + 'was no prior frame to preserve — R1\\'s nav axis does not apply across a full page load');
      } else {
        await clearLog(page);
        await nav(page, route);
        await page.waitForTimeout(2500);
        const back = counts((await readLog(page)).log);
        const bad = [];
        for (const [k, c] of Object.entries(back)) {
          const floor = Math.min(before[k]?.final ?? c.final, c.final);
          if (c.min < floor) bad.push(\`\${k}: dipped to \${c.min} descendants (had \${before[k]?.final ?? '?'}, settled at \${c.final})\`);
        }
        if (bad.length) R(route, 'R1-nav', 'FAIL', \`returning from \${other.route} blanked what was shown — \${bad[0]}\`);
        else R(route, 'R1-nav', 'PASS', \`returning from \${other.route} never dipped below the prior content\`);
      }
    }
    // axis B: filter refetch.
    if (hasFilter && tok) {
      await typeFilter(page, '');
      await page.waitForTimeout(600);
      const pre = counts((await readLog(page)).log.slice(-8));
      await clearLog(page);
      await typeFilter(page, tok);
      await page.waitForTimeout(400);
      const dur = counts((await readLog(page)).log);
      const bad = [];
      for (const [k, c] of Object.entries(dur)) {
        const floor = Math.min(pre[k]?.final ?? c.final, c.final);
        if (c.min < floor) bad.push(\`\${k}: dipped to \${c.min} (had \${pre[k]?.final ?? '?'}, settled at \${c.final})\`);
      }
      if (bad.length) R(route, 'R1-filter', 'FAIL', \`filtering on "\${tok}" blanked the surface — \${bad[0]}\`);
      else R(route, 'R1-filter', 'PASS', \`filtering on "\${tok}" kept prior content on screen\`);
      await typeFilter(page, '');
    }
    R(route, 'R1-write', 'NOT-DRIVEN', 'the post-write refetch axis is not driven — this gate has no '
      + 'app-independent way to perform a write. Rung 3, by design');
  }

  // ── R4 — no inert control ──
  if (want('R4') && hasFilter) {
    const before = await surfaceState(page);
    await typeFilter(page, 'zz' + Math.random().toString(16).slice(2, 10));
    const after = await surfaceState(page);
    if (before && after && before.n === after.n && before.txt === after.txt) {
      R(route, 'R4', 'FAIL', 'a token that can match nothing changed nothing on the surface — the '
        + 'control is inert (no rows change, no explanation appears)');
    } else {
      R(route, 'R4', 'PASS', 'a no-match token visibly changed the surface');
    }
    await typeFilter(page, '');
  }
  if (want('R4')) {
    R(route, 'R4-scope', 'NOT-DRIVEN', 'only the TAGGED filter is driven — a control this build did '
      + 'not tag is invisible to the gate. Under-tagging is the residual hole');
  }

  // ── R5 — in-progress input survives navigation ──
  if (want('R5') && hasFilter) {
    const other = all.find((e) => e.route !== route);
    if (!other) {
      R(route, 'R5', 'NOT-DRIVEN', 'needs a second declared route to navigate to');
    } else {
      const probe = 'nx' + Math.random().toString(16).slice(2, 8);
      await typeFilter(page, probe);
      await nav(page, other.route);
      await page.waitForTimeout(900);
      await nav(page, route);
      await page.waitForTimeout(1500);
      const got = await page.evaluate(() => document.querySelector('[data-surface-filter]')?.value ?? null);
      if (got === probe) R(route, 'R5', 'PASS', \`typed "\${probe}" survived a round trip to \${other.route}\`);
      else R(route, 'R5', 'FAIL', \`typed "\${probe}" did not survive a round trip to \${other.route} (came back as \${JSON.stringify(got)})\`);
      await typeFilter(page, '');
    }
    R(route, 'R5-scope', 'NOT-DRIVEN', 'only the tagged FILTER value is driven — an unsaved form '
      + 'field and the failed-write axis are not. Rung 3, by design');
  }

  // ── R6 — one search idiom app-wide ──
  if (want('R6') && hasFilter && tok) {
    await typeFilter(page, tok);
    const base = await surfaceState(page);
    await typeFilter(page, tok.toUpperCase());
    const upper = await surfaceState(page);
    // Compare the RENDERED state, not just the element count: an empty-state card and a
    // one-row result can carry the same number of descendants, and a count-only compare
    // then reads "case-insensitive" off two surfaces that share nothing but their shape.
    const same = (a, b) => !!a && !!b && a.n === b.n && a.txt === b.txt;
    const caseOk = same(base, upper);
    R(route, 'R6-case', caseOk ? 'PASS' : 'FAIL',
      caseOk ? \`"\${tok}" and "\${tok.toUpperCase()}" return the same surface\`
             : \`"\${tok}" and "\${tok.toUpperCase()}" return DIFFERENT surfaces — the search is case-sensitive\`);
    const acc = accentise(tok);
    if (!acc) {
      R(route, 'R6-accent', 'NOT-DRIVEN', \`"\${tok}" carries no accentable letter — no accent probe exists for this surface\`);
    } else {
      await typeFilter(page, acc);
      const folded = await surfaceState(page);
      const accOk = same(base, folded);
      R(route, 'R6-accent', accOk ? 'PASS' : 'FAIL',
        accOk ? \`"\${acc}" folds to "\${tok}"\` : \`"\${acc}" does not fold to "\${tok}" — the search is accent-sensitive\`);
    }
    await typeFilter(page, '');
  }
}

/** The cross-surface arm of R6: one idiom app-wide means every declared surface lands in
 *  the SAME verdict class. Two surfaces disagreeing is the predicate's defect verbatim. */
function r6Consistency() {
  for (const arm of ['R6-case', 'R6-accent']) {
    const seen = rows.filter((r) => r.pred === arm && (r.verdict === 'PASS' || r.verdict === 'FAIL'));
    const verdicts = new Set(seen.map((r) => r.verdict));
    if (seen.length >= 2 && verdicts.size > 1) {
      R('(app-wide)', \`\${arm}-consistency\`, 'FAIL', 'declared surfaces disagree on this arm — '
        + seen.map((r) => \`\${r.route}=\${r.verdict}\`).join(', ') + '. R6 is one idiom EVERYWHERE');
    } else if (seen.length >= 2) {
      R('(app-wide)', \`\${arm}-consistency\`, 'PASS', \`\${seen.length} declared surfaces agree\`);
    }
  }
}

// ── main run ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
async function run(worktree, urlbase) {
  const prd = path.join(worktree, 'docs', 'PRD.md');
  const entries = fs.existsSync(prd) ? parseReactivityRow(fs.readFileSync(prd, 'utf8')) : null;
  if (!entries) {
    console.log('reactivity gate: reasoned GREEN — docs/PRD.md carries no route-bearing '
      + '\`Reactivity:\` row (nothing to drive; declare one to arm this layer)');
    return 0;
  }
  const { chromium, tried } = await resolveChromium();
  if (!chromium) {
    console.error(\`reactivity gate: playwright not resolvable (tried: \${tried.join(', ')})\\n  fix: \${PW_INSTALL_HINT}\`);
    return 2;
  }
  const browser = await chromium.launch();
  const ctx = await browser.newContext({ viewport: { width: 1512, height: 900 } });
  await ctx.addInitScript(SAMPLER);
  const page = await ctx.newPage();
  try {
    for (const e of entries) await driveRoute(page, urlbase, e, entries);
    r6Consistency();
  } finally {
    await browser.close();
  }

  const fails = rows.filter((r) => r.verdict === 'FAIL');
  const md = ['# REACTIVITY — driven R1–R6 evidence (' + new Date().toISOString().slice(0, 16).replace('T', ' ') + ')',
    '', 'Surfaces: \`' + entries.map((e) => \`\${e.route} \${e.preds.join(',')}\`).join(' · ') + '\`',
    '', '| route | predicate | verdict | detail |', '|---|---|---|---|',
    ...rows.map((r) => \`| \\\`\${r.route}\\\` | \${r.pred} | \${r.verdict} | \${r.detail} |\`),
    '', \`\${fails.length === 0 ? 'GREEN' : \`\${fails.length} FAIL row(s)\`} — RECORDED and NOT-DRIVEN rows carry no verdict by design.\`, ''].join('\\n');
  fs.mkdirSync(path.join(worktree, 'docs'), { recursive: true });
  fs.writeFileSync(path.join(worktree, 'docs', 'REACTIVITY.md'), md);

  if (has('--json')) console.log(JSON.stringify({ ok: fails.length === 0, rows }, null, 2));
  else {
    for (const r of rows) {
      const g = r.verdict === 'FAIL' ? '[31m' : r.verdict === 'PASS' ? '[32m' : '[33m';
      console.log(\`\${g}\${r.verdict.padEnd(10)}[0m \${r.route} \${r.pred} — \${r.detail}\`);
    }
    console.log(\`\\nevidence written: docs/REACTIVITY.md\`);
  }
  return fails.length === 0 ? 0 : 1;
}

// ── SELF-TEST ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Every gated arm gets a planted-bad fixture and must be SEEN RED on it; the GOOD
// fixture must be green on all of them, or a check that always fires would "pass".
// (\`gates-and-ledger.md\`: a guard that has never fired is not a guard.)
const APP = (opts) => \`<!doctype html><meta charset="utf-8"><title>fx</title>
<body><div id="root"></div><script>
const ROWS = ["Brightline Freight","Cargolane Dispatch","Northwind Depot","Solstice Rail",
              "Verity Haulage","Ashgrove Yard","Beacon Transit","Coastal Lines"];
let q = \${opts.dropInput ? 'null' : '""'};
const norm = (s) => { let t = String(s).\${opts.caseSensitive ? '' : 'toLowerCase().'}normalize('NFD')\${opts.accentSensitive ? '' : ".replace(/[\\\\u0300-\\\\u036f]/g,'')"}; return t; };
const match = (r) => \${opts.inert ? 'true' : 'norm(r).includes(norm(q ?? ""))'};
let painted = false;
function render(blank) {
  const route = location.hash || '#/a';
  const root = document.getElementById('root');
  if (\${opts.wordGate ? 'true' : 'false'} && !painted) { root.innerHTML = '<div>Loading…</div>'; painted = true; setTimeout(() => render(false), 260); return; }
  painted = true;
  const list = blank ? [] : ROWS.filter(match);
  root.innerHTML = '<h1>' + route + '</h1>'
    + '<input data-surface-filter value="' + String(q ?? "").replace(/"/g,'&quot;') + '">'
    + '<div data-surface="list">' + (blank ? '' : (list.length ? list.map(r => '<div class="row"><span>' + r + '</span></div>').join('') : '<div class="empty"><span>No matches</span></div>')) + '</div>';
  root.querySelector('[data-surface-filter]').addEventListener('input', (e) => {
    q = e.target.value;
    if (\${opts.blankOnFilter ? 'true' : 'false'}) { render(true); setTimeout(() => render(false), 220); } else render(false);
  });
}
addEventListener('hashchange', () => {
  \${opts.dropInput ? 'q = "";' : ''}
  if (\${opts.blankOnNav ? 'true' : 'false'}) { render(true); setTimeout(() => render(false), 300); } else render(false);
});
render(false);
</script>\`;

const FIXTURES = {
  good:          {},
  'R1-nav':      { blankOnNav: true },
  'R1-filter':   { blankOnFilter: true },
  R2:            { wordGate: true },
  R4:            { inert: true },
  R5:            { dropInput: true },
  'R6-case':     { caseSensitive: true },
  'R6-accent':   { accentSensitive: true },
};

async function selfTest() {
  console.log('SELF-TEST — every gated arm must be SEEN RED on a planted fixture before its green is trusted.\\n');
  const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
    const name = (req.url || '/').split('?')[0].split('#')[0].replace(/^\\/|\\/$/g, '') || 'good';
    const opts = FIXTURES[name];
    res.writeHead(opts ? 200 : 404, { 'content-type': 'text/html; charset=utf-8' });
    res.end(opts ? APP(opts) : 'no such fixture');
  });
  await new Promise((r) => server.listen(0, '127.0.0.1', r));
  const base = \`http://127.0.0.1:\${server.address().port}\`;
  const { chromium, tried } = await resolveChromium();
  if (!chromium) { console.error(\`playwright not resolvable (tried: \${tried.join(', ')})\\n  fix: \${PW_INSTALL_HINT}\`); server.close(); return 2; }

  const drive = async (fixture) => {
    rows.length = 0;
    const browser = await chromium.launch();
    const ctx = await browser.newContext({ viewport: { width: 1512, height: 900 } });
    await ctx.addInitScript(SAMPLER);
    const page = await ctx.newPage();
    const entries = [{ route: \`/\${fixture}#/a\`, preds: ['R1', 'R2', 'R3', 'R4', 'R5', 'R6'] },
                     { route: \`/\${fixture}#/b\`, preds: ['R1', 'R2', 'R4', 'R5', 'R6'] }];
    try { for (const e of entries) await driveRoute(page, base, e, entries); r6Consistency(); }
    finally { await browser.close(); }
    return rows.slice();
  };

  let ok = true;
  const goodRows = await drive('good');
  const goodFails = goodRows.filter((r) => r.verdict === 'FAIL');
  console.log(\`  \${goodFails.length === 0 ? 'GREEN ✓' : 'RED   ✗'}  good fixture\${goodFails.length ? '   ← A CHECK FIRES ON CLEAN INPUT: ' + goodFails.map((r) => \`\${r.pred} (\${r.detail})\`).join(' | ') : ''}\`);
  if (goodFails.length) ok = false;

  for (const arm of Object.keys(FIXTURES).filter((k) => k !== 'good')) {
    const got = await drive(arm);
    const red = got.some((r) => r.pred === arm && r.verdict === 'FAIL');
    const other = got.filter((r) => r.verdict === 'FAIL' && r.pred !== arm).map((r) => r.pred);
    console.log(\`  \${red ? 'RED  ✓' : 'GREEN ✗'}  \${arm.padEnd(11)}\${red ? '' : '   ← FIXTURE DID NOT TRIP THE CHECK'}\`
      + (other.length ? \`   (collateral: \${[...new Set(other)].join(', ')})\` : ''));
    if (!red) ok = false;
  }

  // The STATIC word-gate arm lives in \`guard.sh structural\` CHECK 4; drive it here so
  // one command proves every arm of this gate, wherever the arm is implemented.
  const stat = await staticSelfTest();
  console.log(\`  \${stat ? 'RED  ✓' : 'GREEN ✗'}  R2-static \${stat ? '' : '  ← guard.sh structural did not flag a planted word-gate'}\`);
  if (!stat) ok = false;

  server.close();
  console.log(\`\\nself-test: \${ok ? 'PASS — every gated arm proved it can fail, and none fires on clean input' : 'FAIL — an arm never went red; its green means nothing'}\`);
  return ok ? 0 : 2;
}

async function staticSelfTest() {
  const { execFileSync, } = await import('node:child_process');
  const os = await import('node:os');
  const tmp = fs.mkdtempSync(path.join(os.tmpdir(), 'react-static-'));
  fs.mkdirSync(path.join(tmp, 'src'), { recursive: true });
  fs.writeFileSync(path.join(tmp, 'src', 'Bad.tsx'),
    'export function Bad({ c }: { c: unknown }) {\\n  if (!c) return <div className="content">Loading…</div>;\\n  return <div>ok</div>;\\n}\\n');
  let out = '';
  try {
    out = execFileSync('bash', [path.join(HERE, 'guard.sh'), 'structural', tmp, path.join(tmp, 'src', 'Bad.tsx')],
      { encoding: 'utf8', stdio: ['ignore', 'pipe', 'pipe'] });
  } catch (e) { out = (e.stdout || '') + (e.stderr || ''); }
  fs.rmSync(tmp, { recursive: true, force: true });
  return /word-gate/.test(out);
}

// ── entry ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
if (has('--self-test')) {
  process.exit(await selfTest());
}
const [wt, url] = argv.filter((a) => !a.startsWith('--'));
if (!wt || !url) {
  console.error('usage: reactivity.mjs <worktree> <urlbase> [--json]   |   reactivity.mjs --self-test');
  process.exit(2);
}
process.exit(await run(wt, url));` },
      { path: "references/starter-guide.md", content: `# Starter guide — the org app starter + component library lifecycle

!! Read at P1 whenever an app build starts FROM the org starter (the default base for archetype-3 apps) or hardens a delta back into it (P6 ship event). !!

Two org-owned repos carry the app-building capital. Both are **PRIVATE** (NexusGPT org). The component library is consumed as a **private npm package**, never vendored:

| Repo | Is | Consumed as |
|---|---|---|
| \`github.com/NexusGPT/apps-starter\` → **\`@agent-nexus/apps-starter\`** | the runnable, journey-verified app skeleton for record-system classes (CRM/ATS/ticketing/…): \`starter.config.json\` IS the app (objects · fields · views · relations · permissions · demo rows — \`CONFIG_PATH=<file>\` boots the same build as a different product); a zero-dep node server carries the data spine (command-log replay; warehouse driver) plus the platform wiring; \`.nexus-starter\` marker arms the deploy gate. Its CURRENT capability set is deliberately not listed here — read \`docs/feature-manifest.md\` in the extracted template at build time (one row per shipped feature, with the exact click path and the visible outcome that proves it; \`Last verified\` is stamped by the journey runner) | **\`npm pack @agent-nexus/apps-starter\`** → extract → config-instantiate per app. A PRIVATE package, same as the library: one registry credential reaches both, and no git access is needed to start an app |
| \`github.com/NexusGPT/apps-ui\` → **\`@agent-nexus/apps-ui\`** | the org component library for ANY product: shadcn/ui (new-york-v4) bridged to the Nexus token canvas (\`tokens.css\` = the single restyle surface, light+dark first-class) + the config-driven record-core (table · kanban · chart · record page). Its CURRENT component/prop surface lives in the docs shipped INSIDE the package — never in this file | **\`npm install @agent-nexus/apps-ui\`** — a built, versioned, PRIVATE package. NOT vendored: there is no \`sync-ui\`, no \`src/ui\`, no copy to own. Upgrades are a version bump |

## Import surface

The package's subpaths mirror its source tree, so an import reads as the path it lives at:

\`\`\`ts
import { DataTable } from "@agent-nexus/apps-ui/record-core/DataTable";
import { Button }    from "@agent-nexus/apps-ui/primitives/Button";
import { Wizard }    from "@agent-nexus/apps-ui/blocks/wizard";
import { Input }     from "@agent-nexus/apps-ui/ui/input";   // raw shadcn primitives live under /ui/
\`\`\`

CSS is three imports, in this order, once, at the app entry:

\`\`\`ts
import "@agent-nexus/apps-ui/tokens.css";   // the token canvas — the restyle surface
import "@agent-nexus/apps-ui/shadcn.css";   // tailwind + shadcn base layer
import "@agent-nexus/apps-ui/styles";       // the library's bundled component CSS
\`\`\`

Restyling means overriding the tokens, never editing the library — you no longer own a copy to edit. A control the library lacks is proposed as a LIBRARY addition (ux-canon component-inventory rule), built once in \`apps-ui\`, released, consumed by version bump.

## Navigation contract (drift-proof — BINDING)

Both move fast and are SELF-DESCRIBING; any inventory written outside them (including this file) is stale on arrival. The contract: **capability questions are answered by reading the repo's / the installed package's own truth surfaces at build time, never from memory or skill docs.** Read order per need:

| Need | Read (at build time) |
|---|---|
| entry point + invariants | starter \`AGENTS.md\`, library \`node_modules/@agent-nexus/apps-ui/AGENTS.md\` (the agent-facing front door: what it is, binding rules, command table) |
| what EXISTS right now + how it's proven | starter \`docs/feature-manifest.md\` — one row per feature: exact click path · VISIBLE outcome · runner-stamped \`Last verified\`. The manifest row's \`feature\` string names its journey in \`journeys/run.mjs\` (the executable spec — read the journey when a row is ambiguous) |
| how to USE a capability (config knobs, env flags, recipes) | starter \`docs/RECIPES.md\` (task-shaped how-tos) + \`starter.config.json\` itself (the live example of every config axis) |
| file map / server shape | starter \`docs/ARCHITECTURE.md\` + \`docs/DATA-MODEL.md\` |
| component/prop surface of the library | **\`node_modules/@agent-nexus/apps-ui/docs/\`** — \`INDEX.md\` + \`catalog.json\` (GENERATED — grep the JSON) + \`record-core.md\` (ObjectConfig axes, component contracts, testid conventions) + \`THEMING.md\`. These ship inside the package, so they describe EXACTLY the version installed — no checkout-at-a-different-sha drift. The typed surface (\`dist/**/*.d.ts\`) is the second source of truth. **VENDORED fallback:** when npm auth is unavailable the library often arrives inside an adopted base as a source tree (\`src/ui/\` + a provenance marker such as \`.ui-version\`). Resolution order is installed → vendored; the vendored tree IS the library and its folders (\`record-core/\` · \`primitives/\` · \`blocks/\`) ARE the catalog. Neither resolving = STOP and say so, never proceed as if no library exists |
| contribution rules when MODIFYING either repo (invariants, journey/manifest discipline, multi-agent lane protocol, port bands, suite lock) | starter \`CONTRIBUTING-AGENTS.md\` — binding for any agent lane touching the repos, including parallel-lane builds |
| which version is running / pinning | starter \`VERSION\` · \`GET /api/healthz\` (\`{version, app}\`) · git tags; the library version is whatever \`package.json\` pins (\`node_modules/@agent-nexus/apps-ui/package.json\` → \`version\`) |

Two hard rules: (1) NEVER assert "the starter/library has or lacks X" from recall — the manifest/catalog read is the check (absence = not proven, treat as absent); (2) when the repos and this guide disagree, the repos win — and this guide gets a correction in the same session (flag it).

## Cold start (any machine, no prior state)

Both the starter and the library are PRIVATE npm packages under \`@agent-nexus\`. The \`NPM_TOKEN\` already present in the build environment (read-only, \`@agent-nexus\`-scoped) reaches BOTH — starting an app needs no GitHub access at all.

\`\`\`bash
npm pack @agent-nexus/apps-starter      # → agent-nexus-apps-starter-<v>.tgz
tar -xzf agent-nexus-apps-starter-*.tgz # npm tarballs extract to ./package
mv package <app-name> && cd <app-name>
npm install                             # pulls @agent-nexus/apps-ui from the private registry
npm run build && npm run serve          # zero-dep server: API + built UI
npm run journeys                        # the acceptance surface — green = the manifest's claims hold HERE
CONFIG_PATH=journeys/fixtures/<x>.config.json npm run serve   # same build, different product
\`\`\`

If either package 404s, the token is missing or unscoped — that is an auth failure, never a reason to vendor the library's source, substitute a public component library, or hand-roll a skeleton.

## Lifecycle

1. **Pack → instantiate by CONFIG.** \`npm pack\` the starter → extract → **rename \`package.json\`'s \`name\` to the app and add \`"private": true\`** (the template ships publishable so it can BE a package; an instantiated app must never be publishable) → fill \`starter.config\` (app name, entities, palette seed) → boot. The \`.nexus-starter\` marker file stays — it is what arms the deploy gate's DENY tier (DG1: a starter-born repo shipping with red gate artifacts is 100%-sure unverified) and exempts the repo from BAS1. Never fork-and-drift: an app records the starter VERSION it was born from in \`PROVENANCE.md\`.
2. **Design lock rides the starter.** P0.5 direction boards scaffold from \`boards/\` (three static token-variant boards pre-wired to the app's nouns); the pick commits \`docs/DESIGN.md\`.
3. **Build on the record-core.** Tables/kanban/record pages come from \`@agent-nexus/apps-ui\` — installed OR vendored (resolution order above) — and a missing control is proposed as a LIBRARY addition (ux-canon component-inventory rule), built once, reused everywhere. **Dropping the library is a decision, not cleanup:** removing its imports or stylesheets ("this app renders none of those components") is an inventory verdict that belongs in the plan with its reason, and "a route back to the donor's look" is the misread that costs a rebuild — an org asset carried in by a donor lift is still an org asset.
4. **Journeys from feature 1.** Every feature lands with a manifest row + a journey; \`npm run journeys\` is the canonical Bash-visible runner (the hooks read its artifacts — no MCP-only verification).
5. **Ship-delta (P6).** At ship/accept, the hardened reusable delta (a new wiring module, a component fix, a template improvement) lands as a provenance-tagged commit/PR back to the starter/library — the NEXT build inherits it. A library delta ships as a version bump consumers pick up. The community KB receives the anonymised METHOD entry ONLY — the repos are private, so a KB pointer never carries a URL a reader cannot open. Repo DOCS written on a ship-delta serve the USING agent: functional content only (what exists, how to exercise it) — no build-process narrative, no wave/swarm provenance prose, no internal doctrine vocabulary; \`docs/SPEC.md\` in the TEMPLATE ships empty.
6. **Library upgrades.** Bump \`@agent-nexus/apps-ui\` and re-run journeys — the acceptance surface is what proves an upgrade safe. There is no vendor-sync and no re-vendor step; upstream shadcn tracking happens INSIDE \`apps-ui\`, not in consuming apps.

## Provenance + license posture (BINDING)

- \`PROVENANCE.md\` at each repo root records the per-file origin tags and the carry-scan results. The code is MIT-licensed but distribution is PRIVATE (org repos + a restricted-access npm package) — private distribution does not relax any origin rule below.
- EVERY file carries an origin tag in \`PROVENANCE.md\`: **\`ours\`** (authored from scratch — carried/de-branded freely) · **\`rebuilt\`** (clean-room reauthored from written specs/ideas — ideas are not copyrightable; the spec is cited) · **\`derived\`** (traces to third-party source). MIT/ISC-derived (shadcn/Radix patterns) is fine WITH notices kept in-repo. Copyleft-derived (AGPL/GPL) may exist ONLY as a temporary, tagged scaffold and MUST be replaced by \`rebuilt\` before ANY client delivery — identifier-stripping does not change a derivative's license.
- A carry from a prior fork workstream runs the CARRY SCAN first: \`git log --diff-filter=A --author\` to list files WE created; grep each against the donor's source for pasted fragments; only clean \`ours\` files cross, de-branded. The scan output is committed to \`PROVENANCE.md\`.

✗ "strip the donor's identifiers and repackage" → structural similarity is trivially detectable against public donor source, and a repo being private is not a defence once code ships to a client. → ✓ ideas + layouts carry as SPECS, our own files carry after the scan, the UI rebuilds on MIT primitives — ~90% of the value, zero legal surface.` },
      { path: "references/ux-canon.md", content: `# UX canon — information architecture + surface craft for hosted apps

Gate: read before the FIRST user-facing unit of EACH surface (SKILL P3; a compaction voids this read — R#13). The P0.5 design lock (\`docs/DESIGN.md\`) is the surface's visual AUTHORITY — read it WITH this file; this canon is the floor under whichever direction was picked.
\`frontend-craft.md\` owns rendering MECHANICS (does it work); this file owns DESIGN QUALITY (is it right).
Every rule below shipped as a user rejection once.

## The spine — glance → zoom → act

A management surface answers three questions in order, and its layout IS that order:

1. **GLANCE** — priority legible in one screen: a ranked/triaged board (compute the urgency score explicitly), status visible per row WITHOUT expanding (colored dot, count chip, progress bar).
2. **ZOOM** — one interaction to detail: accordion row or side drawer; NEVER a tab-jump for contextual detail (the jump survives as a secondary action INSIDE the drawer).
3. **ACT** — actions live where the reader already is: in-row/in-panel, persisted immediately, state reflected in place (checkbox → progress → counter).

✗ a flat fully-expanded list, everything the same weight, actions elsewhere → "layout is not readable, needs full overhaul" → ✓ scored triage board → one-line rows w/ verdict dots → accordion detail → in-place actions. Density is MEASURED: an open detail ≈ one screen (a 3-screen detail = restructure; two-column w/ a sticky context rail is the usual fix).

## Surface checklist (run per NEW surface, before first render)

| Item | Why (each shipped as a rejection once) |
|---|---|
| Explicit TYPE SCALE — every text element styled (title ~13.5/700 · body ~12.5/400 · meta 12 · micro-labels 10/800 caps); measure ≤~82ch | unstyled elements inherit the 16px browser default next to 13px neighbors = "font size inconsistency" |
| ONE nav pattern app-wide (sub-tabs: same component, same position on every tab) | two nav grammars = "inconsistent between tabs" |
| COMPONENT INVENTORY before any new control — the app's best existing component (filter bar, card, chips) is GENERALISED + tweaked, never re-invented per surface; org-wide the inventory ROOT is \`@agent-nexus/apps-ui\`, **RESOLVED against this repo's real shape**: INSTALLED (\`node_modules/@agent-nexus/apps-ui/docs/catalog.json\`) **or VENDORED** (a component tree carrying a provenance marker — e.g. \`src/ui/.ui-version\`; a library that arrived with a donor lift is still THE LIBRARY, never "the donor's look"). Read the resolved root, never recall. **A root that does not resolve is a STOP, not a pass** — a check that cannot execute reports green while every control is re-authored beside a superior one. The rule binds SUBTRACTION too: dropping a library import or stylesheet is an inventory decision, not cleanup. A control missing from the resolved root is proposed as a LIBRARY addition, not built as a one-off | two bespoke filter systems next to a superior existing one = "reuse components instead of reinventing the wheel"; a vendored library imported 8 times out of 20,688 LOC while its equivalents are hand-written |
| Stat chips that look clickable ARE clickable (they write into the same filter model) | count-styled-as-button that does nothing reads as broken |
| Long enumerations (>12 options) get typeahead; active filters surface ONE clear-all chip | a 400-option dropdown is unusable |
| AI results land ON the originating artifact (closed loop) and AI output is EDITABLE in place (stable anchors so structural edits never desync human feedback; armed 2-click delete) | send-side-only features + read-only AI output = "we need to see the result clearly on the plan" |
| Micro-interactions, sober set: expand transition, chevron rotate, checkbox pop, hover lift, \`:focus-visible\` (never the default ring) | static surfaces read as unfinished; default focus rings read as broken |
| Bounded internal scroll for long user content inside cards (max-height + overflow, thin scrollbar) | one long review stretching a card breaks the board |
| i18n: the language switch re-renders the ACTIVE view; every new string born in ALL dicts (count defs × languages); DATA stays in its own language | chrome switches, content doesn't = "page doesn't change language" |
| MOBILE (~≤760px) before ship: stacked cards, 2-line list rows, scrollable chip rails, full-width CTAs, no horizontal scroll (guard it), tap targets ≥40px; the @media block lives at stylesheet END (later same-specificity base rules win) + verify at 390px programmatically | desktop-only ships = "doesn't feel like an app on mobile" |
| Banner rhythm: balanced padding, centered search, nav underline offset from the edge | "margin in top banner is bad" |
| Irreversible or outward actions (send, delete, book, pay, bulk mutate) get a REVIEW surface before the fire: exactly what will happen (target, content, scope) shown + editable/redirectable + cancellable, and a verifiable result state after | a one-click real send with no preview or recipient control = "no confirmation, no message, unclear what is sent" |
| Icons from an ESTABLISHED library (e.g. Lucide, MIT), vendored as SVGs for self-containment; hand-drawn only for the brand's own marks | a hand-built icon set reads as amateur and swapping late touches every surface |
| Config/control surfaces speak the USER'S domain language — qualitative levels + concrete factors whose inputs the user can discover/supply; raw internal weights/thresholds/enums exposed as "settings" read as fake precision | decimal-weight sliders over abstract dimensions = "fake accuracy", unusable |

An org with its OWN design canon (brand/design-system files in its workspace) supersedes these defaults — read it FIRST when it exists (R#47); this checklist is the floor, not the ceiling.

## Uncertain hypothesis → ask, don't ship

When the IA hypothesis is not obviously forced (which grouping, which default view, fixed vs composable), state it as a one-line hypothesis to the user BEFORE building — a 30-second contrast question beats a rebuilt surface (Dim 8; design-conversation-canon "Surface-shape probe").

## Irreversible-action surfaces (canonical for CLAUDE.md G#9 builder's mirror)

The tool-execution tier discipline (READ / REVERSIBLE / SOFT-REVERSIBLE / IRREVERSIBLE) governs any UI Cue BUILDS: a user-triggered action that is irreversible or leaves the product (send/write/booking/payment/delete/bulk mutation) ships WITH a review surface — exactly what will happen: target, content, scope; edit/redirect/cancel — plus a verifiable result state after. A bare fire-button is the ✗ form, whatever the action.` }
